angr opens libz3 itself: library_dir() takes the z3-solver wheel's bundled lib directory and
load() dlopen()s a fixed "libz3.so". Two things are wrong on Arch. That directory does not
exist, because Arch packages z3-solver as Python files only and keeps the library in the
separate z3 package under a different soname; and the wheel's Rust extension has a hard
DT_NEEDED on libz3.so.5.3, which no packaged z3 provides. Importing angr therefore dies with

    OSError: .../site-packages/z3/lib/libz3.so: cannot open shared object file

Carry the exact library angr was built against inside this package (angr/_z3lib, reached from
the extension through an $ORIGIN rpath) and fall back to it. That directory holds only the
versioned soname, so load() has to accept it as well.

Both branches keep upstream behaviour when the wheel's own layout is present, so this stops
mattering once the repos ship z3-solver 5.3.3.0 or newer.

diff --git a/angr/_z3.py b/angr/_z3.py
index f9a18aa..c57174a 100644
--- a/angr/_z3.py
+++ b/angr/_z3.py
@@ -27,9 +27,18 @@ def library_dir() -> Path:
     spec = importlib.util.find_spec("z3")
     if spec is None or spec.origin is None:
         raise ImportError("angr requires the z3-solver package, which does not appear to be installed")
-    return Path(spec.origin).parent / "lib"
+    bundled = Path(spec.origin).parent / "lib"
+    if (bundled / LIBRARY_NAME).is_file():
+        return bundled
+    return Path(__file__).parent / "_z3lib"
 
 
 @functools.cache
 def load() -> ctypes.CDLL:
-    return ctypes.CDLL(library_dir() / LIBRARY_NAME)
+    directory = library_dir()
+    library = directory / LIBRARY_NAME
+    if not library.is_file():
+        versioned = sorted(directory.glob(f"{LIBRARY_NAME}.[0-9]*"))
+        if versioned:
+            library = versioned[0]
+    return ctypes.CDLL(library)
